11.text:
For one instrument the first stage t–ratio is $t_{1}=\sqrt F$ and, up to
$o_{p}(1)$,

\begin{equation*}
t_{1}\;\approx\;N\!\left(\sqrt{\mu^{2}},1\right) ,\qquad 
\mu^{2}\equiv n\,\frac{\pi^{2}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}},
\end{equation*}
is the concentration parameter.  On the Nagar approximation

\begin{equation*}
{\rm plim}\;\frac{{\rm bias}\big(\hat\beta_{2SLS}\big)}
{{\rm bias}\big(\hat\beta_{OLS}\big)} \simeq\frac{L}{\mu^{2}},\qquad
L=1,
\end{equation*}
so that, with the usual normalisation, the relative Nagar bias can be
written as

\begin{equation*}
b(\mu^{2})\simeq \frac{1}{\mu^{2}}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence $b(\mu^{2})=0.10$ is obtained for

\begin{equation*}
\mu^{2}_{0}=1/0.10=10 ,\qquad \sqrt{\mu^{2}_{0}}=\sqrt{10}\approx3.16 .
\end{equation*}
A conventional $95\%$ lower confidence bound for the non-centrality
$\sqrt{\mu^{2}}$ is

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{\hat\mu^{2}_{L}}=t_{1}-z_{0.95}\;, \qquad 
z_{0.95}=1.645,
\end{equation*}
so that $\hat\mu^{2}_{L}=(t_{1}-z_{0.95})^{2}$.  Requiring
$\hat\mu^{2}_{L}\ge \mu^{2}_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
t_{1}-z_{0.95}\ge\sqrt{\mu^{2}_{0}},\qquad 
t_{1}\ge\sqrt{\mu^{2}_{0}}+z_{0.95},
\end{equation*}
gives the one–sided $95\%$ critical first-stage $t$–ratio

\begin{equation*}
t_{1}^{*}\approx\sqrt{10}+1.645\approx4.81,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
F^{*}=t_{1}^{*2}\approx\bigl(\sqrt{10}+1.645\bigr)^{2}\approx
2.3\!-\!2.4\times10^{1}\approx 23
\end{equation*}
for a one–sided $95\%$ lower bound.  With the usual two–sided
$z_{0.975}=1.96$ this is $t_{1}^{*}\approx\sqrt{10}+1.96\approx5.12$,
$F^{*}\approx 26.2\approx26$, the familiar $F\approx 1.96^{2}/0.10$
conservative bound.

In particular $E\,b\simeq1/F$, so that on an expected–value basis
$E\,b\approx0.10$ is $F\approx10$, the usual Staiger–Stock rule of
thumb $F\gtrsim10$ for $\approx10\%$ relative Nagar bias.  The
$9\!-\!10$ Stock–Yogo $10\%$ maximal–relative–bias critical value is
$F_{SY}\approx8.96\approx9$, essentially the $E\,b=0.10$ rule
$F\approx10$.

