11.text:
For a just identified homoscedastic model with $k_{2}=1$
\begin{equation*}
{\rm RelBias}\equiv
\frac{{\rm E}\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta}{{\rm E}\hat\beta_{OLS}-\beta}
\approx\frac{k_{2}}{\mu^{2}}\approx\frac1{\mu^{2}}\approx\frac1{F-1}
\approx\frac1{F},
\end{equation*}
and in the weak instrument limit
\begin{equation*}
kF\;\xrightarrow{d}\;\chi^{2}_{k}(\mu^{2}),\qquad 
\sqrt{F}= \sqrt{\mu^{2}}+Z+o_{p}(1),\;Z\sim N(0,1),
\end{equation*}
so that on the $\sqrt F$ scale
${\rm sd}\big(\sqrt F\big)\approx 1$.  Putting
${\rm RelBias}=b$ the Nagar point is $\mu^{2}_{0}=b^{-1}$.  For
$b_{0}=0.10$,
\begin{equation*}
\mu^{2}_{0}=10,\qquad \sqrt{\mu^{2}_{0}}=\sqrt{10}\approx 3.16 .
\end{equation*}
A one sided $95\%$ lower confidence bound for $\sqrt{\mu^{2}}$ is
\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{\hat\mu^{2}_{L}}=\sqrt F-z_{0.95},\qquad z_{0.95}=1.645,
\end{equation*}
so that $\hat\mu^{2}_{L}\ge\mu^{2}_{0}$ iff
\begin{equation*}
\sqrt F\ge \sqrt{\mu^{2}_{0}}+z_{0.95} .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the $\mu^{2}\approx F$ normalisation the $5\%$ weak instrument
critical value for the $10\%$ Nagar relative bias is
\begin{equation*}
F_{0.95}^{*}= \Big(\sqrt{\mu^{2}_{0}}+z_{0.95}\Big)^{2}
          =\Big(\sqrt{10}+1.645\Big)^{2}\approx 4.81^{2}\approx 2.3\!-\!2.4\times10^{1},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $F^{*}\approx 22\!-\!24$ and in round numbers $F^{*}\approx23$.
On the conventional $E F =1+\mu^{2}$ normalisation
$\mu^{2}=F-1$ this is
$\sqrt{F-1}\ge\sqrt{10}+1.645$, i.e. $F-1\approx21\!-\!23$,
$F\approx22\!-\!24$, the familiar $F\approx10$ point Nagar rule
$1/F\approx0.10$ inflated by the $z_{0.95}$ sampling error in $\sqrt F$.
With the usual two sided $z_{0.975}=1.96$ this is $F^{*}\approx
(\sqrt{10}+1.96)^{2}\approx26$.  This is the $F\approx10$ point rule
$F>10$, $F-1>10$, up–rated to the $95\%$ lower confidence bound for
$\mu^{2}$.  In the operational Stock–Yogo size-distortion calibration the
same Nagar first order bias is inverted to a $5\%$ maximal relative bias
test and is conventionally quoted as
$F_{SY}(1,1;10\%)\approx16.4\approx16\!-\!17$, i.e. the familiar
$F\gtrsim10$ rule of thumb $F\gtrsim16$ in practice. ∎

